'''Lake Pend Oreille''' ({{IPAc-en|ˌ|p|ɒ|n|d|ə|ˈ|r|eɪ}} {{respell|POND|ə|RAY}})<ref>{{Cite web|title=Definition of pend oreille {{!}} Dictionary.com|url=https://www.dictionary.com/browse/pend-oreille|access-date=June 29, 2020|website=www.dictionary.com|language=en}}</ref> in the northern [[Idaho Panhandle]] is the largest [[lake]] in the [[U.S. state]] of [[Idaho]] and the [[List of largest lakes of the United States by area|40th-largest lake by area]] in the United States, with a surface area of {{convert|148|sqmi}}. It is {{convert|69|km}} long, and {{convert|1152|ft}} deep in some regions, making it the fifth-deepest in the nation and having a volume of 43,939,940 acre feet = 54&nbsp;km<sup>3</sup>. The lake is fed by the [[Clark Fork River]] and the [[Pack River]], and drains into the [[Pend Oreille River]], as well as [[Subsurface flow|subsurfacely]] into the [[Spokane Valley–Rathdrum Prairie Aquifer]].<ref name=USGS />
It is surrounded by [[United States National Forest|national forests]] and a few small towns, with the largest population on the lake at [[Sandpoint, Idaho|Sandpoint]]. The majority of the shoreline is non-populated and all but the southern tip of the lake is in [[Bonner County]]. The southern tip is in [[Kootenai County]] and is home to [[Farragut State Park]], formerly the [[Farragut Naval Training Station]] during [[World War II]], of which a small part is still active and conducts [[United States Navy|U.S. Navy]] [[acoustics|acoustic]] underwater submarine research.<ref>{{cite news |last=Geranios |first=Nicholas K. |date=August 23, 1998 |title=Small Submarines Test Silent, Test Deep in Mysterious Idaho Lake |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1998-aug-23-me-15711-story.html |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=January 10, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.navy.mil/navydata/cno/n87/usw/issue_11/submarine_stealth.html |title=Small Subs Provide Big Payoffs for Submarine Stealth |access-date=February 6, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130313053142/http://www.navy.mil/navydata/cno/n87/usw/issue_11/submarine_stealth.html |archive-date=March 13, 2013 }}</ref>

The surrounding forests consist of [[ponderosa pine]], [[Douglas fir]], [[Thuja plicata|red cedar]], [[Populus|poplar]], [[quaking aspen]], [[Tsuga|hemlock]], [[paper birch]] and [[western larch]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fs.usda.gov/photogallery/ipnf/learning/nature-science/gallery/?cid=1185&position=Quicklinks|title=USDA |access-date=September 28, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.idahoforests.org/trees1.htm |title=Trees of The Idaho Forest |access-date=August 12, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110826070103/http://www.idahoforests.org/trees1.htm |archive-date=August 26, 2011 }}</ref>  Local animal species include [[white-tailed deer]], [[elk]], [[gray wolves]], [[moose]], [[mice]], [[squirrels]], [[chipmunks]], [[American black bear|black bears]], [[grizzly bear]], [[coyotes]], [[mountain goat]], cougar and [[bobcats]], along with [[bald eagles]], [[wild turkeys]], [[osprey]], [[owls]], [[hummingbirds]], [[hawks]], [[woodpeckers]], [[ducks]], and the [[mountain bluebird]]. The lake is a home for several species of migratory water fowl.

== History ==

Lake Pend Oreille was glacially formed during the [[ice age]]. It is also believed that the eastern side of the lake was in the path of the ancient [[Missoula Floods]]. The lake sits at the south end of the Purcell Trench,<ref>{{Cite web|title=GSA Bulletin {{!}} GeoScienceWorld|url=https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/gsabulletin|access-date=September 30, 2021|website=pubs.geoscienceworld.org}}</ref> carved by glaciers moving south from Canada. The eastern side of the glacier is believed to have formed the dam for the Missoula floods, at the point where the Clark Fork river enters the lake between the Cabinet and Bitterroot mountains. The lake is made slightly larger by the dam at [[Albeni Falls Dam|Albeni Falls]], just east of [[Oldtown, Idaho|Oldtown]]; the dam is {{convert|90|ft|spell=in}} high and operated by the [[United States Army Corps of Engineers|U.S. Army Corps of Engineers]]. Along with [[Crater Lake]], [[Lake Tahoe]], [[Lake Chelan]], and [[Lake Superior]], Lake Pend Oreille is among the five deepest lakes in the United States.

The area around the lake is the traditional home of the Kalispel Indian peoples. [[David Thompson (explorer)|David Thompson]] established a [[North West Company]] trading post on the lake in 1809. A Canadian fur trader in Thompson's party is believed to have given the lake its name. The words "Pend Oreille" are French for an ear-hanging or pendant. Ear pendants were characteristic of the Kalispel tribe. The lake is shaped much like a human ear when viewed from above or on a map. In 1846, Great Britain ceded the bulk of its claims to the Pacific Northwest pursuant to the [[Oregon Treaty]].

[[File:US Navy 051130-N-7676W-033 The Advanced Electric Ship Demonstrator (AESD), Sea Jet, undergoes sea trials on Lake Pend Oreille at the Naval Surface Warfare Center Carderock Division, Acoustic Research Detachment in Bayview, Idah.jpg|thumb|left|The [[Sea Jet|Advanced Electric Ship Demonstrator (AESD)]] undergoes sea trials on Lake Pend Oreille at the Naval Surface Warfare Center]]

During [[World War II]], the south end was [[Farragut Naval Training Station|the second largest naval training ground]] in the world and the largest "city" in the state.  Built as a result of the [[Imperial Japanese Army|Japanese]] attack on [[Pearl Harbor]], the training station is now [[Farragut State Park]]. The lake's Large Scale Vehicle Range is still used by the [[United States Navy|Navy]]'s Acoustic Research Detachment for [[sonar]] testing with large-scale [[submarine]] prototypes on the Pend Oreille Calibration Station. The significant depth gives acoustic properties similar to the open ocean.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.dt.navy.mil/about_us/division_sites/acoustic_research_detachment/acoustic_research_detachment.html |title=Acoustic Research Detachment |access-date=February 3, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080201010945/http://www.dt.navy.mil/about_us/division_sites/acoustic_research_detachment/acoustic_research_detachment.html |archive-date=February 1, 2008 }}</ref> Background noise is less than at ocean testing locations, and the sound signatures being investigated cannot be monitored by foreign governments in international waters.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://foxtrotalpha.jalopnik.com/the-navys-most-vital-and-secretive-submarine-base-is-in-1590794426 |title=The Navy's Most Vital And Secretive Submarine Base Is In... Idaho?!? |last=Rogoway |first=Tyler |website=Foxtrot Alpha |date=June 14, 2014 |publisher= |access-date=April 24, 2018 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.navsea.navy.mil/Home/Warfare-Centers/NSWC-Carderock/Who-We-Are/Bayview-Idaho/ |title=Acoustic Research Detachment - Bayview, Idaho |last= |first= |website=[[Naval Sea Systems Command]] |publisher=[[United States Navy]] |access-date=April 24, 2018 }}</ref>

In 1967, the former Farragut Naval Training Station was used to accommodate the 12th World Scout Jamboree (August 1 to 8), the dates being significant as it marked 60 years to the date after Sir Robert Baden Powell held his first scout camp on Brownsea Island in Poole Harbour, Dorset in England.

==Other information==
[[File:Map_of_the_Sandpoint_Idaho_Region.png|thumb|The highways and cities on the northern end of Lake Pend Oreille]]

The following towns are adjacent to the lake:

*[[Sandpoint, Idaho|Sandpoint]]
*[[Ponderay, Idaho|Ponderay]]
*[[Kootenai, Idaho|Kootenai]]
*[[Hope, Idaho|Hope]]
*[[Clark Fork, Idaho|Clark Fork]]
*[[Bayview, Idaho|Bayview]]
*[[Sagle, Idaho|Sagle]]

The lake is viewed from the [[Pend Oreille Scenic Byway]], [[Idaho State Highway 200|State Highway 200]] along the north shore.  [[U.S. Route 95|US 95]] crosses the lake via Long bridge. Much of the shore is mountainous. The mountains to the east are the Green Monarchs; to the north are the [[Cabinet Mountains]]; to the southwest the [[Coeur d'Alene Mountains]]; and to northwest are the [[Selkirk Mountains]].

==Aquatic invasive species==
The lake is known to harbor several [[invasive species]]. Some of these species include:

* [[Myriophyllum spicatum|Eurasian watermilfoil]]
* [[Walleye]] (Also known as Yellow Pike)
* [[Lake Trout]]
* [[Northern Pike]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lake Pend Oreille |url=https://idfg.idaho.gov/ifwis/fishingplanner/water/1163468481594 |access-date=April 25, 2026 |website=Idaho Fishing Planner |publisher=Idaho Fish and Game |language=en}}</ref>

==Fish==
[[File:Bull trout sign at Lake Pend Oreille.jpg|thumb|Sign in Sandpoint for anglers regarding protection of bull trout]]

The lake is home to many species of fish, including [[rainbow trout]], [[lake trout]], [[bull trout]], [[cutthroat trout]], [[brown trout]], [[cutbow]], [[perch]], [[black crappie]], [[bluegill]], [[largemouth bass]], [[smallmouth bass]], [[freshwater whitefish|whitefish]], [[walleye]], [[northern pikeminnow]], and [[kokanee salmon]]. Lake Pend Oreille was also the home of the {{convert|37|lb|adj=mid|abbr=off}} state record gerrard rainbow trout (aka kamloops rainbow) caught by Wes Hamlet in 1947.<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=June 15, 2017|title=Certified Weight Fish Records|url=https://idfg.idaho.gov/fish/record/certified-weight/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200623210908/https://idfg.idaho.gov/fish/record/certified-weight|archive-date=June 23, 2020|access-date=June 29, 2020|website=Idaho Fish and Game|language=en}}</ref>

